#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501357 :question/problem "Let $a = ⟨-24,6⟩$. Find $\\mid a \\mid$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid a \\mid &= \\sqrt{a_{x}^{2} + a_{y}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(-24)^{2} + (6)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{576 + 36} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{612} \\\\\n&= 6 \\sqrt{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501363 :question/problem "If $v = ⟨20,-5⟩$, then what is the magnitude of $v$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid v \\mid &= \\sqrt{v_{x}^{2} + v_{y}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(20)^{2} + (-5)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{400 + 25} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{425} \\\\\n&= 5 \\sqrt{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501365 :question/problem "If $v = ⟨8,-2⟩$, then what is the magnitude of $v$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid v \\mid &= \\sqrt{v_{x}^{2} + v_{y}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(8)^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{64 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{68} \\\\\n&= 2 \\sqrt{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501373 :question/problem "If $v = ⟨16,-4⟩$, then what is the magnitude of $v$?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid v \\mid &= \\sqrt{v_{x}^{2} + v_{y}^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(16)^{2} + (-4)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{256 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{272} \\\\\n&= 4 \\sqrt{17}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501385 :question/problem "![](images/07/07a59129f6186959fa9ebad8b924177a81910a3d25c2f292169c41474df99a31.png)\n\nLet $A(-12, 3)$ and $B(9, 0)$. Find $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|$." :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OA} &= a = - 12 i + 3 j, \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{OB} &= b = 9 i.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB} &= \\overrightarrow{OB} - \\overrightarrow{OA} \\\\\n&= b - a \\\\\n&= (9 i) - (- 12 i + 3 j) \\\\\n&= 21 i - 3 j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\overrightarrow{AB}| &= \\sqrt{(21)^{2} + (-3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{441 + 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{450} \\\\\n&= 15 \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501390 :question/problem "![](images/56/5682cc802577e4dd43f42e03bfc829658fc96ca83a15422aa079fe2b43258a0c.png)\n\nLet $A(-8, 2)$ and $B(6, 0)$. Find $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|$." :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OA} &= a = - 8 i + 2 j, \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{OB} &= b = 6 i.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB} &= \\overrightarrow{OB} - \\overrightarrow{OA} \\\\\n&= b - a \\\\\n&= (6 i) - (- 8 i + 2 j) \\\\\n&= 14 i - 2 j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\overrightarrow{AB}| &= \\sqrt{(14)^{2} + (-2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{196 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{200} \\\\\n&= 10 \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501391 :question/problem "![](images/57/57fd993fac384d9dd76399aa35eec0d9113afeb3c1d24c0a5349d7b4b99df4f1.png)\n\nLet $A(-16, 4)$ and $B(12, 0)$. Find $|\\overrightarrow{AB}|$." :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OA} &= a = - 16 i + 4 j, \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{OB} &= b = 12 i.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{AB} &= \\overrightarrow{OB} - \\overrightarrow{OA} \\\\\n&= b - a \\\\\n&= (12 i) - (- 16 i + 4 j) \\\\\n&= 28 i - 4 j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\overrightarrow{AB}| &= \\sqrt{(28)^{2} + (-4)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{784 + 16} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{800} \\\\\n&= 20 \\sqrt{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501407 :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7b3f6503172aeab9c4554ffac492a9aab8c16e9ce14f5c552cd1d89dcaa3c66.png)\n\nLet $P(-4, 4)$ and $Q(6, 6)$. Find $|\\overrightarrow{PQ}|$." :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &= p = - 4 i + 4 j, \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{OQ} &= q = 6 i + 6 j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{PQ} &= \\overrightarrow{OQ} - \\overrightarrow{OP} \\\\\n&= q - p \\\\\n&= (6 i + 6 j) - (- 4 i + 4 j) \\\\\n&= 10 i + 2 j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\overrightarrow{PQ}| &= \\sqrt{(10)^{2} + (2)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{100 + 4} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{104} \\\\\n&= 2 \\sqrt{26}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501414 :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bd80d88ece15997923e5860ff1328dea542c2950c9e4399eba6a5e872070f18.png)\n\nLet $P(-6, 6)$ and $Q(9, 9)$. Find $|\\overrightarrow{PQ}|$." :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{OP} &= p = - 6 i + 6 j, \\\\\n\\overrightarrow{OQ} &= q = 9 i + 9 j.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing $\\overrightarrow{PQ}$ as the difference of position vectors, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\overrightarrow{PQ} &= \\overrightarrow{OQ} - \\overrightarrow{OP} \\\\\n&= q - p \\\\\n&= (9 i + 9 j) - (- 6 i + 6 j) \\\\\n&= 15 i + 3 j\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|\\overrightarrow{PQ}| &= \\sqrt{(15)^{2} + (3)^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{225 + 9} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{234} \\\\\n&= 3 \\sqrt{26}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501424 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $2a+b$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a+b &= 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0+(2) \\\\ (-4)+(13)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|2a+b| &= \\sqrt{(2)^2+(9)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{85} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{85}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $2a+b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{85}}\\cdot (2a+b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{85}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{2 \\sqrt{85}}{85} \\\\ \\frac{9 \\sqrt{85}}{85}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501425 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $2a+b$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a+b &= 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -17\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0+(20) \\\\ (-4)+(-17)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|2a+b| &= \\sqrt{(20)^2+(-21)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{841} \\\\\n&= 29\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $2a+b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{29}\\cdot (2a+b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{29}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}20 \\\\ -21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{20}{29} \\\\ - \\frac{21}{29}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501435 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $2a+b$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a+b &= 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0+(4) \\\\ (-6)+(-1)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|2a+b| &= \\sqrt{(4)^2+(-7)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{65} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{65}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $2a+b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}\\cdot (2a+b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{65}}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -7\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{4 \\sqrt{65}}{65} \\\\ - \\frac{7 \\sqrt{65}}{65}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501436 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $2a+b$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a+b &= 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0+(-12) \\\\ (-2)+(-3)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|2a+b| &= \\sqrt{(-12)^2+(-5)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{169} \\\\\n&= 13\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $2a+b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{13}\\cdot (2a+b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{13}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-12 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}- \\frac{12}{13} \\\\ - \\frac{5}{13}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501437 :question/problem "Let $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 38\\end{bmatrix}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $2a+b$ and points the same direction." :question/worked-solution "Remember that to find a unit vector that is parallel to a given vector and points the same direction, we need to divide the given vector by its length:\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute the sum:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a+b &= 2\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 38\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 38\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0+(9) \\\\ (2)+(38)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for the magnitude of a vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n|2a+b| &= \\sqrt{(9)^2+(40)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1681} \\\\\n&= 41\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we divide $2a+b$ by its length and get the following unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{2a+b}{|2a+b|} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{41}\\cdot (2a+b) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{41}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 40\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{9}{41} \\\\ \\frac{40}{41}\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502096 :question/problem "Consider a small rectangle $Δ$ whose bottom-left corner is located at the point $(3,1)$ with sides parallel to the coordinate axes in the $uv$-plane and whose area is $\\delta A.$ Which of the following gives the best approximation for the area of the image of this rectangle under the action of the transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}⟶\\begin{bmatrix}{u}^{3}-v \\\\ v-u\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the absolute value of the Jacobian determinant gives the local area scale factor of the transformation at that point:\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(T(Δ))≈|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\cdot \\underbrace{\\text{Area}(Δ)}_{\\delta A}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the corresponding Jacobian determinant, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂x}{∂u} & \\frac{∂x}{∂v} \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂u} & \\frac{∂y}{∂v}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}3{u}^{2} & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}\\cdot 1-(-1)\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =3{u}^{2}-1 \\\\ {\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|}_{(3,1)} & =(3{u}^{2}-1)\\,{|}_{u=3\\,v=1} \\\\  & =3(3)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(T(Δ)) & ≈|26|\\cdot \\delta A \\\\  & =26\\,\\delta A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502222 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $30$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{30}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502223 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $\\frac{13}{8}$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{13}{8}}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\frac{8}{13}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502225 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $\\frac{17}{8}$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{17}{8}}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\frac{8}{17}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502232 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $11$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{11}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502236 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $2 \\sqrt{13}$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{2 \\sqrt{13}}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{26}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502240 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $\\frac{7}{6}$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{7}{6}}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\frac{6}{7}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502241 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $\\frac{9}{4}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{9}{4}}\\mathbf{a}=\\frac{4}{9}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502242 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $23$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{23}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502251 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $3 \\sqrt{7}$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{3 \\sqrt{7}}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\frac{\\sqrt{7}}{21}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502260 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $16$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{16}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502272 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $17$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{17}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502278 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $3 \\sqrt{11}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{3 \\sqrt{11}}\\mathbf{a}=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{33}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502283 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $\\frac{18}{7}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{\\frac{18}{7}}\\mathbf{a}=\\frac{7}{18}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502297 :question/problem "The vector $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ has magnitude $5 \\sqrt{15}$. Which of the following is a unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$?" :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, we divide $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\overrightarrow{AB}}{\\mid \\,\\overrightarrow{AB}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{5 \\sqrt{15}}\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\frac{\\sqrt{15}}{75}\\overrightarrow{AB}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502319 :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{a}$ has magnitude $4 \\sqrt{13}$. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To obtain the unit vector that is parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$, we divide $\\mathbf{a}$ by its length:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}=\\dfrac{\\mathbf{a}}{\\mid \\,\\mathbf{a}\\, \\mid}=\\dfrac{1}{4 \\sqrt{13}}\\mathbf{a}=\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{52}\\mathbf{a}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502334 :question/problem "![](images/d6/d62b7f63213e0a36a4cc186c396de6986f8c9e594fa045791d9c37fad7550f60.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/ae/ae31ae08afde8173954eef7609108f51e2a5113e7ef37a815511d29e653f33bf.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=0.5$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{5}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502340 :question/problem "![](images/35/35ce1ab5c1689dca152c58e955e4a43c0be0e2bc6a796bf20e84f2c99786f8ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/9b/9b906579f40ce2a1c59129d80540b339c1bb00fcd9a5f5ce179b01efeb702ab5.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=3.5$ and $BH=0.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{49}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{5 \\sqrt{2}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502342 :question/problem "![](images/11/1112fef974be645940a11f8f3f099ca6b0125a6a92ca8642712dde7b86c63ac8.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/4c/4c8930d8535e42d793def554d8e65943e637c02debb6d411048096ba1b8cda68.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=2$ and $BH=0.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(2)^2+\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{4+\\frac{1}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{17}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{17}}{17}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502356 :question/problem "![](images/36/364b9ef322e1d6c1aaa54e23a2c09bf086f3721370da7b69aa7036222d1e4318.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/89/894f70223b9b29220ee424f53c1ae82ff804fc9b7ca3638e43481d7cea63f66a.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=5$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(5)^2+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{25+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{109}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{109}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{109}}{109}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502358 :question/problem "![](images/42/42b28384b26d180eb7ac4fb945a1e75659961be7a4eae1aaed42abe2c861b3ca.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/93/93cd69372903948a36ea13c7db78c1f2df59db19cdb882089263896d7313432e.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=1$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(1)^2+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{13}}{13}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502385 :question/problem "![](images/1d/1dbd27c1b4987c7b6196da934b586f93062d5e9a77144a0c9defb3f8db9adf17.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/e8/e81324960632400a3af894d472f054a9e5035dc77ccf8ce87b6720d5af2d5b2f.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=0.5$ and $BH=3$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)^2+(3)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}+9} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{37}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{37}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{37}}{37}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502393 :question/problem "![](images/da/daa0c47f022b83ba8fe6cd059f7a2971ee92082c9b315f813c93df806b743377.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/ac/ac6e9bcd576c30cd721babe0d12a31cd9a3ba0d4905ad3b02e32d1dd8ca012c9.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=1.5$ and $BH=3.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}+\\frac{49}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{58}}{29}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502397 :question/problem "![](images/02/02112c027add54e9bc7412151f3bd04c31d33e51eb1f96aded05b59e2489950e.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $c$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/d4/d4f6e1f602a4bf57830c11f0814f2fb7d6d1c2304421a32284f6bfb5a92690b5.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=4$ and $BH=1.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(4)^2+\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16+\\frac{9}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $c$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{73}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|c|}\\cdot c \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\sqrt{73}}{73}c\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502398 :question/problem "![](images/b2/b25f39d9bbd30e928d8e07defbed4b2c3e3c4ef46ed92186fb83372dac14b1fd.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $a$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "![](images/d6/d61b907e6fe5abc9c3e1f3197bfbfc24b7fc67b2e70366877d71d284abf16e85.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=4$ and $BH=3$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{(4)^2+(3)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{16+9} \\\\\n&= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $a$ is $5$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|a|}\\cdot a \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{5}a\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186502399 :question/problem "![](images/13/1313a6787b3d2da5ddf917ad9cad72791bc465a265dd73cac8fe9c806afc74ec.png)\n\nConsider the picture shown above. Find a unit vector that is parallel to $m+n$. Choose the unit vector in the same direction." :question/worked-solution "First, we have to find $m+n$. According to the triangle law of addition, the resultant $m+n$ goes from the tail of $m$ to the head of $n$:\n\n![](images/fe/fe7fb47997949a3bbf0699ee771a1a05f8c8d7c6558140719d7f7a00316fc231.png)\n\nIn $\\bigtriangleup AHB$ we have $\\angle AHB=90^\\circ$, $AH=4.5$ and $BH=2.5$. So, the Pythagorean theorem gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nAB &= \\sqrt{AH^2+BH^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right)^2+\\left(\\frac{5}{2}\\right)^2} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{\\frac{81}{4}+\\frac{25}{4}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{106}}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the magnitude of $m+n$ is $\\frac{\\sqrt{106}}{2}$. Consequently, the unit vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu &= \\frac{1}{|m+n|}\\cdot (m+n) \\\\\n&= \\frac{\\sqrt{106}}{53}(m+n)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
